<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ennius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">239–169 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Quintus Ennius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quintus Ennius was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poet, born in 239 BCE at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudiae">Rudiae</a> in the Messapian south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by Cato, he wrote tragedies, comedies and satires, and his epic Annales, telling the history of Rome in hexameters, made him known as the father of Latin poetry. He died in 169 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ennius.jpg" alt="Ennius" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wolfgang Sauber · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptothek_-_Doppelherme_Vergil-Ennius.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
